{"timestamp": 1778703522.8668025, "duration_sec": 2.5512571334838867, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Every day, thousands of people, mostly American, mostly white, and mostly retired, come to this town looking for relief from the turmoil in their mouths. Here, dental care costs up to 80% less than what it might cost in the US. Dental tourism to Los Algodones is so common that many call this place Molar City. Today, there are nearly 1,000 dentists in this 7,000-person town, and 98% of those in the patient's chair traveled here from outside Mexico. Lasha is going to take it from here."], [1, "Watch any one of these YouTube videos, and you'll see how a trip to Molar City is made easy for an American. For decades, this crossing didn't even require a passport. In one video, a retired couple arrives at Arizona's edge. They do a 360, displaying the massive lot where they've just parked. \"We love Mexico,\" they say emphatically, talking to the camera. The camera then pans to show the rust-colored border wall in front of them. That corrugated monstrosity. They marvel. Then a US Border Patrol vehicle starts driving past the screen, on guard, it seems, for anyone trying to cross in the opposite direction. To this, the camera woman speaks again, as if she's talking directly to Border Patrol. \"Get 'em, boys,\" she says. Then they begin their walk into Mexico. In Los Algodones, there are dense clusters of medical offices and a canopy of billboards above them. Vendors are set up on sidewalks where they sell handmade goods and kitschy souvenirs, like this one graphic tee that says, \"Keep calm, you're on the fun side of Trump's wall.\" And then there are the street promoters, people who are stationed at almost every street corner. Each of them competing for superlatives: the best deal, the quickest appointment, the most popular clinic in town. In Molar City, a street promoter is the first person a tourist will encounter."], [2, "Good morning, buddy."], [3, "What is it? Dental?"], [1, "Pharmacy?"], [3, "Glasses?"], [2, "I got dental promotion."], [1, "Some promoters try to lure people in with discounts. Others vie for attention in more creative ways. Like dressing up in a full-body tooth costume and waving a sign. Then there's Alberto, a street promoter who takes a different approach altogether. He tries to attract customers with humor."], [3, "I'm always trying to make stuff funny, you know, make them laugh like a comedian almost, make them relax just for that second."], [1, "Alberto is employed by a couple clinics in town. He's assigned to a specific street corner where he stands all day and tries to make his commissions."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental tourism", "los algodones", "molar city", "affordable dental care", "american retirees", "cross border travel", "mexico clinics", "dentists", "street promoters", "youtube videos", "border wall", "medical offices", "healthcare industry", "marketing tactics", "tourism economy", "humor communication", "us mexico relations", "competition"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703651.8790505, "duration_sec": 4.469498634338379, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nLibraries, Education, and\nNeighborhoods Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nWednesday, April 22, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:32 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Rivera\nPresent: 5 - Maritza Rivera, Joy Hollingsworth, Dionne Foster, Eddie Lin,Alexis Mercedes Rinck\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. Appt 03492 Appointment of Quynh Pham as Director of the Department of\nNeighborhoods, for a term to December 31, 2029.\nThe Appointment (Appt) was discussed.\nE. Adjournment\nMeeting Adjournment Time: 10:15 a.m.\nPrepared by: Nicholas Duda\nPage 1"], [0, "Squeezed between high costs and low revenue, small businesses are taking on debt, putting off bill payments and laying off staff as they struggle to stay afloat. - Facebook\nJan 15, 2026 \u00b7 Downtown Seattle: Often 35\u201339%+ (e.g., nearing 35% in Q2 2025, up to 39.1% in some Q4 2025 data, with submarkets like Pioneer Square over 50%). Missing: districts | Show results with: districts"], [1, "How Small Town High Streets Are Outpacing City Centres in Retail ...\nUrban high streets , once dominated by department stores and large chains, now face widespread closures and vacant spaces. However, small towns are experiencing a retail renaissance driven by localism, community engagement, and innovative retail formats. What\u2019s fueling this\u2026"], [2, "Home -- EuroShop - World\u00b4s No. 1 Retail Trade Fair - Next event: Feb...\nExperience EuroShop 2026 live: Discover global retail trends , expert insights and innovations shaping the industry. Stay ahead and explore the highlights now!"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "libraries education and neighborhoods committee", "department of neighborhoods", "councilmember maritza rivera", "councilmember joy hollingsworth", "councilmember dionne foster", "councilmember eddie lin", "councilmember alexis mercedes rinck", "quynh pham", "nicholas duda", "seattle", "city hall (seattle)", "600 4th avenue seattle wa 98104", "downtown seattle", "pioneer square", "facebook", "euroshop 2026", "euroshop", "appt 03492"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703922.1477876, "duration_sec": 2.2250266075134277, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Everyone talked about the Jet Center. Yeah, man, that was\u2014 So we were."], [1, "Always wondering, like, what is the style? What's the best style? And then the UFC came along and I'm like, oh my God, they did it. They did it. They figured it out. They put it all together. And for a while it was jiu-jitsu because nobody understood jiu-jitsu and Royce Gracie was just running shit."], [0, "Well, you know, you know how that was kind of set up a little bit."], [1, "It was a little set up."], [0, "Yeah, yeah, yeah."], [1, "But I mean, look, he had some challenges. Like Ken Shamrock was a beast."], [0, "Oh yeah."], [1, "He had some really good fighters he was facing against. Kimbo Slice. I mean, excuse me, not Kimbo Slice."], [0, "Not Kimo."], [1, "Kimo."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn",