{"timestamp": 1778703994.7614589, "duration_sec": 2.0505611896514893, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "I'm always trying to make stuff funny, you know, make them laugh like a comedian almost, make them relax just for that second."], [1, "Alberto is employed by a couple clinics in town. He's assigned to a specific street corner where he stands all day and tries to make his commissions."], [3, "Since I'm like at a corner, I have a lot of people that pass by. So I talk to everybody. 8 hours out there, I'm over. From early in the morning, I'm talking and talking and I don't stop talking all day."], [1, "He told me that street promoters can also get a little territorial with each other because they're all vying for the attention of the same Americans who walk into town."], [3, "You don't need appointment, my door just open right now."], [1, "You get that service right away."], [3, "We're not working with point men."], [2, "Good morning, buddy. What is it?"], [3, "Dental?"], [1, "Pharmacy?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["humor", "comedy", "street promotion", "marketing", "sales", "customer service", "dental clinic", "pharmacy", "medical tourism", "walk in service", "territorial competition", "street corner", "communication", "persuasion", "small business", "healthcare industry", "tourism", "work fatigue"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778704264.8238304, "duration_sec": 2.0413196086883545, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "This was my first party. Look at American people, how they doing just in the street, you know? I don't have places to do it."], [1, "Medina flips through the book fondly, as if he's cooing at baby photos."], [2, "And that's the way Algodones start, you know, with that. And like this, you can see all my book is full of parties, you know, to where I go and everything, you know. Well, it's all my life in this book, you know."], [1, "It's clear that Medina put his heart into these parties, into making the town grow the way it did. He convinced his neighbors to cook enough food to feed thousands for free. He hired a mariachi band and converted an old boxing dressing ring into a stage. And the Americans had a great time. Medina made sure the margaritas were strong."], [2, "And they was drunk like crazy, you know. The American customs told me, Dr. Medina, why you give to those guys?"], [0, "Why?"], [2, "They cross right here, they don't want to show the papers, they tell us son of a bitch and go."], [3, "They was very drunk."], [1, "Year after year, Los Salgadones would continue to throw parties like that first one. Americans would return home, talk of the tacos, the town, their teeth. What was once a town of about 750 when Maga\u00f1a first arrived is now crammed with clinics, hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies. Today, young dentists and souvenir vendors from all over Mexico continue to migrate to town in hopes of finding stable work. And the tourists keep coming in droves."], [2, "In winter, it's too much people and they make lines, and that's uncomfortable for them, but they don't care. They make the lines, you know, and they still coming because they find dental services good, a good price and everything, and very handsome doctors. We live from the tourists, you know."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental tourism", "border town", "mexico", "american tourists", "street parties", "community events", "free food", "mariachi music", "margaritas", "drinking", "customs officers", "local economy", "hospitality industry", "migration", "small business", "healthcare services", "cross border travel", "cultural exchange"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778704382.8114333, "duration_sec": 2.057621479034424, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Oh, this is so\u2014 I mean, I have the CSM collection. Yeah, I know. This list just looks amazing."], [1, "So Red Corsair Raiders are OC2. Oh, that's interesting. And they Infiltrate. Okay, were there any good infiltration options in CSM before this unit?"], [0, "Uh, no, but you have a redeploy with the Masters of the Maelstrom, so that's kind of nice with the Infiltrating Corsairs."], [1, "That is nice. Yeah, I love that. That's awesome. And if they kill a unit, you, uh, and it's not Battleshock, you get +1 OC. So I take it you're probably going for the redeploy here, but a little unit that can advance and charge and kill some guys on turn 1 is pretty nice, like, you know, just to not get move blocked."], [0, "Pretty expensive, this unit, to be fair. That\u2014 how much are they?"], [1, "120? 110?"], [0, "110? Is that expensive? Because 55 points for 5 is actually not bad, dude. This is a unit of 10."], [1, "No, no, this is a unit of 5."], [0, "A unit of 5 for 110 sounds."], [1, "Expensive, but they are 3 wounds apiece."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer", "chaos space marines", "red corsair raiders", "infiltrate", "redeploy", "objective control", "points cost", "unit size", "wounds", "advance and charge", "turn one tactics", "movement blocking", "tabletop wargaming", "game balance", "list building", "strategy planning", "miniature gaming", "competitive play"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778705070.957663, "duration_sec": 2.4628427028656006, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That is, uh, okay, that's one thing, but this is what kills my soul about the UK, right? The second someone goes, oh, here's a really easy build to use, Why are there 17 Custodes players who probably have 51 Land Raiders? And you know, you know, the Emperor made what, 100 of these, or 1,000 of these Custodes? They're all here, they're in Manchester, they're all there in Manchester."], [1, "Yeah, but that's crazy when you think about it, right? Like, look how, look how thick the narrow the middle is at the top, right? Like, you've got Necrons, which is probably playing about 80 C'tan total, and those 25 players Death Guard, and then Custodes as well. I mean, that's crazy, right? Like, you need a lot of damage to get through this meta at the moment, and you don't really need a lot of chaff units, right? Like, you're gonna have to go through your opponents in these games, I think. Uh, Chaos Knights still at 10, uh, CSM only at 9. So I don't know if the new rules for CSM have come out, uh, but, um, we've got some lists at Pirate Cup that are playing the new CSM rules as well, so we could show you a few lists. And You know what, interestingly enough, this is actually really interesting. Look at Guard. Guard is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7. It's 8th equal, which\u2014 yeah, wow, popular. You know, interestingly enough, 8th equal is very low for Guard historically because Guard has actually been very high up in the play numbers for UKTC events in general. I would say usually it's in that 1-2-3 position, I think. So I think a lot of people are struggling to get through these C'tan with Guard, and that's, that's the same with me. I was\u2014 it was a slog to kill these Necrons, mate. And yeah, with losing the reroll ones, I think it's even harder."], [0, "So Dave, there was a\u2014 and this is an interesting one that I would love to go and backtest\u2014 there was a period of time where you and Nassim were running Guard at the same time. Consistently tournament after tournament. I feel like Guard were not popular before that, and they progressively became popular as you guys kind of almost led or pushed the community towards stronger Guard lists, and more people jumped in when they saw this is competitively viable. Now with both of you and generally kind of the top players not playing Guard, they're, they're kind of trying everything else. I wonder if we see a reversal here because I personally think that the Guard rules are still stronger than a lot of the other techie armies in on the list there, but you're right, they're a little underrepresented here."], [1, "Yeah, you know, these UKTC boards are not great for Guard in some ways because Guard\u2014 so what was really interesting was these, you know, Grizzle Company came out and it had the ability to order Bullgryn, right? And people immediately went, oh, Bullgryn are going to be insane, like, you know harkening back to the old days. Uh, turns out that was not true. And actually, you know, as soon as you removed the Bullgryn from your list, your list became significantly stronger, right? Because your list became too narrow, and Guard doesn't like these narrow list archetypes, really. And so, uh, what that does is\u2014 I've lost my train of thought here, but no, um, but Bullgryn turns out\u2014 oh yeah, here's what I was going to say. Bullgryn, uh, not great. And these UKTC boards, uh, were quite heavy So actually maneuvering around some of these pieces without access to something like Advance and Shoot on UKTC boards, if you're going to play a Rogal Dorn for example, is actually quite hard. So I think that's the big limiting factor, right? Like, look, the Rogal Dorn is the best unit in Guard, I think, and like Scions are good and can fill that gap, but it's, uh, you're gonna have these turns where you just have to\u2014 your opponent puts a Necron, a C'tan out there, You have to commit, and if you just whiff, you instantly lose the game. You don't have full access to fall back and shoot, of course. And C'tan now having the 3+ save is brutal for so many armies, because if you get a cover save and you're, and you're getting shot by AP-1, then you're taking 3-ups instead of 4-ups, which is just brutal. So I, you know, I've played the 27-man block, uh, and I was playing that in my list, but with the reroll ones gone to wound, I was probably gonna have to go back to 2 Rogal Dorns for the damage output. But playing 2 Rogal Dorns on UKTC is so tough. So yeah, it was, uh, it was\u2014 yeah, it's interesting. I mean, I, I'm really happy I went with Ultramarines for this event, and I just felt like it gave me a lot more play into every single army. I wasn't going to be put in positions where, hey, my opponent just played a solid turn 1, and okay, if my multi-melters don't connect into this C'tan, then it's going to be a tough game, right? So that's, uh, that's kind of where I was at with it. Um, and I think Ultramarines just gives me more flexibility going over. Yeah, yeah. Only 9 players as well."], [0, "Oh, fall from grace, fall from grace. How could this happen? But you know what, I think Aeldari players are finding their feet. You're going to be, uh, seeing a lot of people who are just flitting into Eldar going back out of Eldar, uh, and left with the core player base, which is actually a pretty large core player base. Um, I think just a bit of guidance is needed. I mean, I've just published that video and I had my list in the video, so I'm hoping that people try and are like, oh, wait a second, you know, we can still make this work in, in something that's competitively viable. And I hope that slightly increases the player rate for Aeldari over the next few months."], [1, "Question, Vic, uh, what, what's the story with these new units and these new detachments with Aeldari? What was going on there? Was there anything spicy there, anything good?"], [0, "Uh, it's not as spicy as the CSM stuff, honestly. Uh, the rules are significantly weaker, but like, let's say, you know, you could pay 75 points for a 12-inch moving jump pack unit that just\u2014 that doesn't do anything else. It has a couple of guns and stuff. In my books, that's quite good because that's where my standards are at the moment. Um, and we've got that now, so we have like an okay choice to add a little bit of tech. We have a really fragile gunboat that doesn't actually shoot very much, but it's only 75 points. So it's kind of like we have 1 or 2 pieces that we can just add to our lists, but we have nothing that significantly changes where Aldar sits. And the detachments are so thematic for Corsairs that one of them gives you like an 18-inch lone op strat for one of your infantry units. Which is really cool, but Seer Council already does that, and it does better across all the other stratagems. So you could run almost the same list from your Seer Council list, uh, from your, uh, Corsair list as Seer Council, and probably tweak it to be significantly better. So I don't think we're going to see any changes really, except for people adding in one Skyreaver or Starfang to their list."], [1, "Okay, that's interesting. It's, it's interesting that they decided to develop kind of the same mid-range units for Aeldari. It would have been cool to see something more at the higher end there, like the Wraithlord type archetype or something like that, you know."], [0, "They're, they're a bit stuck, to be honest. I mean, uh, we can do a little wishlist on balance changes, but I, I think they're stuck now because community sentiment hates Eldar being, um, having the tricksy style play style. But also they always get the defensive play style wrong as well, so they can never really find the midpoint. And maybe Eldar best suited being like this, where they're a little bit weaker and people having to work hard to try and get something out of the army. I think they stay out of the limelight, and Eldar players can still really enjoy the game at the moment. You actually don't want your faction to be the one that's winning loads and loads of tournaments. It's not a safe spot to be in."], [1, "So, longevity\u2014 that's interesting. Yeah, the, uh, should we\u2014 why don't we go over\u2014 I guess we'll, we'll look at some CSM lists here as well."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer 40000", "uktc events", "tournament meta", "army lists", "adeptus custodes", "land raiders", "necrons", "ctan", "death guard", "astra militarum", "chaos space marines", "aeldari", "balance changes", "terrain layout", "community sentiment", "competitive strategy", "ultramarines", "game design"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
