{"timestamp": 1778707311.8848212, "duration_sec": 1.9196722507476807, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "The president says he's all about getting bargaining power. Well, imagine calling the Chinese and saying, I demand concessions or else I'm going to put this 10% tariff on you. And I'm pretty sure the Chinese know how to wait 150 days and then say, you know what, mate, there's nothing left. Or it could be the president says he's going to onshore manufacturing. But I tell you what, I've talked to some factory owners. No one's going to break ground on a new factory when the tariffs are off."], [0, "before the concrete's even set. What you're left with here actually is a 10% tariff that performs no role except being a tax. So what we have is a pro-tax president. Yeah, and a tax that again,"], [0, "Our very founders fought kings to not be taxed without representation. Here we are all these years later. So much has changed. Not that, apparently. And Bloomberg is already reporting the tariff ruling will kick off a fight for what is a serious chunk of change, because as Neil Kochel was explaining, they let this play out for strategic reasons. And the high court has been very friendly to Trump in letting him kind of break"], [0, "laws on the short run. I mean, this is now as of today illegal, but yesterday they were taking this money. So $170 billion with a B is out there, companies filing suits, companies want their money back. Of course, they were able to sue precisely because they were paying and harmed by this. How will that process play out? How much that money is actually going to come back into those pockets?"], [0, "I know you care deeply about the numbers here. I think the number is going to end up being closer to $130 billion, but it doesn't really matter. I actually think this is an interesting rhetorical point, but not that interesting for the economics. Why? Well, first of all, it's all about what happened in the past. And the question is, does the money end up in the government's coffers or in our pockets? If it's not in our pockets and the government needs money, it's going to reach back into our pocket and take it back out. The bad news for people at home is you and I aren't going to see any of this."], [0, "Why is that? Well, I buy my olive oil from Costco. It's imported. Costco imported the olive oil. Therefore, Costco in the first instant paid the tariff. That's who has a claim here. Now, Costco jacked up the price and I had to pay more for my olive oil, which is deeply disappointing."], [0, "But Costco's not going to call me and say, Justin, we noticed that you might have paid a little extra, but that was just the price we wanted to charge you. So this whole fight is basically about whether Costco or other American importers get the money or the government pockets the money, but you and I haven't seen any of it."], [0, "If that's the case, you have a Trump government that jacks up those prices, some of it paid, as you say, on the way. And if they passed off some of it to consumers, then we don't get any of that money back. They're not going to track us down and give us that. They're going to pay it off to the companies. How was any of this good for the American consumer?"], [0, "Mate, it's not. So look, I know you're the lawyer here, but here's a very simple rule of thumb. If you're going to institute a policy that's going to upend your own economy, the global economy, foreign relations, and change our position in the world, probably best to check that it's constitutional first. Right. Just hint for beginners. So they created them as sort of strategically on purpose, making it harder to undo all of this. So the trade war was bad."], [0, "But having a trade war that you were never allowed to actually run is even worse. This is literally before against tariffs. This is the dumbest thing you could possibly do. It means we get all the costs and none of the benefits. It's really extraordinary. And it's a massive scale. And you think about people worried about inflation and high prices and jobs. This is another form of a price gouge. If in the end, it was all for naught. And this was a president who ran on what? He ran on"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["tariffs", "trade war", "china relations", "bargaining power", "manufacturing onshoring", "factory investment", "taxation", "importers", "consumer prices", "inflation", "court ruling", "illegality", "refund lawsuits", "constitutional law", "global economy", "foreign policy", "supply chains", "price gouging", "corporate profits"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707530.907942, "duration_sec": 4.64213228225708, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "September 10, 2025\nA regular meeting of the City Council of the City of Boston was held in the Christopher A. Iannella\nChamber, City Hall on Wednesday, September 10, 2025, at 12:05 P.M.\nPresident Louijeune in the Chair. Present Councilors Breadon, Durkan, Coletta Zapata, FitzGerald,\nFlynn, Mejia, Murphy, Pep\u00e9n, Santana, Weber, and Worrell.\nBishop Homicil, Voice of the Gospel Tabernacle, delivered the invocation, and the meeting was opened\nwith the pledge of allegiance.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the minutes from the August 27, 2025, meeting.\nMotions prevailed.\n_________________________________________________________________________\n1619 Message and order for your approval an order to reduce the FY26\nappropriation for the Reserve for Collective Bargaining by Fifty-Five\nMillion Eight Hundred Ninety-Nine Thousand One Hundred Eighteen\nDollars ($55,899,118.00) to provide funding the Boston Public Schools\n(BPS) for the FY26 impact of increases contained within the collective\nbargaining agreem"], [0, "Boston City Councilors to Discuss Re-Legalizing Triple-Deckers\nBut the excitement evident among Boston City Council meeting Wednesday showed the door might be cracking open to re- legalizing three - and four -family buildings in many parts of the city."], [1, "Boston council hearing spotlights plan to legalize triple ...\nNov 18, 2025 \u00b7 Public testimony included residents who described triple deckers and ADUs as multigenerational housing lifelines; other commenters urged attention to fire\u2011access and setbacks and warned about institutional investors targeting single\u2011family lots."], [2, "CitizenPortal.ai - Boston councilors, police and retailers ...\nOct 17, 2025 \u00b7 Small-business witnesses and trade groups told the council that lost sales, rising security costs and damaged storefronts are threatening thin retail margins and neighborhood retail diversity."], [3, "Shoplifting rose 30% in Boston last year. What\u2019s being done ...\nFeb 9, 2025 \u00b7 Shoplifting in Boston rose 30% last year, according to city crime data. The spike was the primary driver behind a 2% increase in property crime, Police Commissioner Michael Cox said in..."], [4, "Prevent Theft in Retail - By Petrosoft\nImprove your c-store's bottom line. Trusted by thousands of US convenience stores. CStoreOffice back-office software overcomes your c-store hurdles. Request a free demo! Reduce Inventory Loss \u00b7 100% Remote Set-Up \u00b7 Increase C-Store Revenue \u00b7 Integrate with POS system Service catalog : Back-office solutions, Loss Prevention Analytics, SmartPOS"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston city council", "boston", "boston public schools", "police commissioner michael cox", "president louijeune", "councilor breadon", "councilor durkan", "councilor coletta zapata", "councilor fitzgerald", "councilor flynn", "councilor mejia", "councilor murphy", "councilor pep\u00e9n", "councilor santana", "councilor weber", "councilor worrell", "bishop homicil", "voice of the gospel tabernacle", "reserve for collective bargaining fy26 $55", "899", "118", "citizenportal.ai"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707546.8818905, "duration_sec": 1.8983228206634521, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "en route to Cuba, the first due to arrive Monday. Mexico has also indicated it is trying to find ways to resume selling oil to Cuba after stopping earlier this year due to U.S. pressure. For NPR News, I'm Katie Silver in Mexico City. This is NPR News."], [0, "Hundreds of demonstrators rallied in Damascus, Syria today. They're protesting efforts to restrict the sale and consumption of alcohol. They fear Syria's new Islamist government may threaten long-held secular freedoms. The interim government has been under pressure from hardliners to impose more conservative religious values in Syria."], [0, "The rally attracted residents from a range of religious sects. One told the Associated Press, this is not about whether we want to drink alcohol. This is about personal freedom. Los Angeles Lakers superstar LeBron James has set another career NBA record. Steve Futterman reports it came in Florida last night as the Lakers beat the Orlando Magic."], [0, "In his 23rd season, 41-year-old LeBron James set a new record for longevity and durability. James played in his 1612th game. LeBron sets the record for most games played regular season, and here he is, swishing it through for two."], [0, "The previous record, 1611, was held by Robert Parrish, who played 21 seasons. James holds dozens of records, including the most prestigious, most points ever scored, more than 43,000. In Saturday's record-setting game for most games played, the Lakers beat Orlando by one point. For NPR News, I'm Steve Futterman in Los Angeles."], [0, "In men's college basketball, both No. 1 seeds, Duke and Michigan, advanced yesterday to the next round. The Nebraska Cornhuskers moved to the Sweet 16 for the first time in its history, beating Vanderbilt 74-72. I'm Nora Rahm, NPR News in Washington."], [0, "Kenny Boone just hired a new chef who is very consistent. So far, he never came late. I asked him for overtime. He never said no to me. Does he never complain? Never complain. That new hire is a robot. Robots are coming for the restaurant industry. What that means for the food we eat. Listen to Planet Money on the NPR app or wherever you get your podcasts."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cuba", "mexico", "oil exports", "united states pressure", "damascus", "syria", "protests", "alcohol restrictions", "islamist government", "civil liberties", "nba", "restaurant robots", "international relations", "energy trade", "religious conservatism", "secularism", "sports records", "automation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
