{"timestamp": 1778708669.982238, "duration_sec": 2.1194212436676025, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Sigue a Mariela Rolt\u00e1n en Instagram. Encu\u00e9ntrala como arroba Mariel Rolt\u00e1n. Y en arroba noticias gui\u00f3n bajo Milet. Super est\u00e9reo Milet, la radio con poder."], [0, "Y lo usar\u00e9 solo para decirte lo mucho que lo siento. Que si me ven con otra en una disco solo es perdiendo el tiempo. Baby, \u00bfpa' qu\u00e9 te miento? Eso de que me vieron feliz no lo es cierto. Ya nada me hace re\u00edr."], [0, "Solo cuando veo las fotos y los videos que tengo de ti Sal\u00ed con otra para olvidarte Y ten\u00eda el perfume que te gusta a ti Prendo para irme a dormir Porque duermo mejor si sue\u00f1o que est\u00e1s aqu\u00ed Si supieras que te escrib\u00ed Me han dado los mensajes, siguen todos ah\u00ed, wow Que mucho me ha costado Quiz\u00e1s te hice un favor cuando me fui de tu lado"], [0, "Borracho viendo tus fotos, me duele ver que t\u00fa se has mejorado No tienes d\u00edas grises, ya no te duelen las cicatrices Y yo pensando si decirte que me queda un por ciento Y lo usar\u00e9 solo para decirte lo mucho que lo siento"], [0, "Que si me ven con otra en una disco solo es perdiendo el tiempo Baby pa' que te miento Eso de que me vieron feliz no, no es cierto"], [0, "Hace tiempo no pensaba en ti Borracho, t\u00fa viste, me met\u00ed Baby, ya yo s\u00e9 que a ti te va bien Que de m\u00ed t\u00fa no quieres saber, ey, ey Viviendo en un infierno que ya mismo incendia Jugando contigo como si fuese el d\u00eda Siento que ya no estoy en tu coraz\u00f3n"], [0, "Ahora estoy en tus pies rog\u00e1ndote En el tequila ahog\u00e1ndome Los muchachos est\u00e1n invit\u00e1ndome a salir, la paso bien Pero siempre termino extra\u00f1\u00e1ndote En el tequila ahog\u00e1ndome, ey Las morritas testi\u00e9ndome, ey Quedando en la peda hoy, pero Me quedo un porciento"], [0, "Y lo usar\u00e9 solo para dec\u00edrtelo mucho que lo siento. Que si me ven con otra en una disco, estar\u00e9 perdiendo el tiempo. Baby, pa' qu\u00e9 te miento. Eso de que me vieron feliz no es cierto. Y eso es Grupo Frontier."], [0, "Ser amable y servicial crea unidad en la humanidad."], [0, "Cuanto m\u00e1s puras son mis intenciones, mayor es el beneficio de todas mis acciones. \u00bfMe reviso, ayudo con alegr\u00eda o lo hago nada m\u00e1s por cumplir? Esa es mi pregunta. Entonces descubro que la felicidad de dar con sabidur\u00eda"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["instagram", "radio promotion", "song lyrics", "heartbreak", "regret", "jealousy", "alcohol", "tequila", "nightclubs", "photos", "missing someone", "apologies", "latin music", "social media marketing", "entertainment industry", "emotional healing", "generosity", "moral reflection"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708768.376618, "duration_sec": 1.658069372177124, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "thing was trending or memeing or whatever. And it just went like, oh man, that's such a sad place to be because you're just playing yourself, to quote is a callet. You're playing yourself, man. You're playing yourself when you don't show up and deliver the maximum of your personal capacity, not for the business sake, although that's a nice side effect. And if you work in a nice business with nice coworkers, hopefully eventually you will be recognized, but that's not why you do it."], [0, "You do it for your own progress, your own sense of identity and move forward. Now, again, if you're working a dead end manual labor job, that's a different category and kettle of fish. And I can see how people arrive at an actual wage thing. But I saw it creep in to creative endeavors."], [0, "people thinking, you know what, creatively, I should hold back. It doesn't work. That's not how the pie is cut. You will grow the pie. The more you stretch yourself, the more you show up with everything you have, not in terms of hours, again, this isn't about hours, but in terms of dedication and pursuit of impact and all these other things, you will grow your pie if you're using all of it. You cannot save energy. You can't go like, oh, I'm just going to coast this week. Then next week,"], [0, "I'm going to have double. No, you're not going to have double. You're going to have less. This is one of the things that were really fascinating to me early on in my career, where I sort of kind of acted this way at at least one employment that I vividly remember, where it's just like I didn't really like the people who were running the company. I didn't really like how things were assigned. And I thought, like, you know what? I'm just going to I'm going to coast a little."], [0, "And I realized that the coasting was hurting one person more than anyone else. And it was me. I had less energy when I came home from coasting a whole day. I had less energy the next week. It was a downward trend. And I think after that experience, I really went like, you know what? It doesn't matter who otherwise benefits from me showing up with my best all the time. I'm going to benefit."], [0, "Me, most of all, I will have more energy to do things after hours If I put in the maximum Creativity is sort of a regenerative force The more of it you use, the more of it you'll have So investing in that sense of your own capacity By using all of it as much as you can Is just a wise strategy for yourself and your own career development"], [0, "Okay, last question before we wrap it up, because we've been talking about work ethic and not just being about hours. As you're hiring, someone who's listening is like, yeah, I want to bring on people who have a great work ethic. How do you find those people? Because in the hiring process, it's not just like, tell me about your work ethic. And we know it's not just about hours. Do you have any tips as you're looking to grow your team, finding people who, you know, do what they say they're going to do and follow through and all of those things that we say go beyond just the hours? Yeah."], [0, "I think work ethic is a trickier thing to interview for, but I think that you can definitely ask references about that. That's been my technique for that. I typically don't put a whole lot of weight in references in general because people are providing them and they're probably going to be good, but you can ask around the edges and find some of those answers. But I think you can also ask people about, you know, ask them to walk you through some of their work and you can sort of prod at that"], [0, "With a work ethic lens and sort of ask a bit about how you approach this project. What was it like? Who else did you work with? How was that? There's some questions you can get to around those edges. But I don't know. In my opinion, it's not the easiest thing to dig into in terms of interviewing. Maybe David has a different approach. But I found that to be..."], [0, "Usually someone else has a better insight into your abilities in terms of your showing up and your timeliness and your willingness to help and your willingness to run into problems than you typically do. I think everyone else has a better sense of that than you might yourself, especially in an interview situation where you're going to probably give the answers that you want someone to hear. So anyway, that's my feeling. And maybe it's something that's more judged at that six-month mark or one-year mark than it is in the interview process."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["work ethic", "creative work", "personal growth", "motivation", "career development", "dedication", "productivity", "coasting", "energy management", "workplace culture", "hiring", "interviewing", "reference checks", "leadership", "performance evaluation", "organizational behavior", "self identity", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709023.2787263, "duration_sec": 2.1487669944763184, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "Agua Dulce was a very small town with lots of cantinas, and used to come people from Yuma to dance in the cantinas or drink."], [1, "This is Dr. Jesus Medina. He moved to town fresh out of med school in 1973, and he's been working here as a doctor ever since, although he seems to be perpetually on the brink of retirement."], [2, "I think one day I have to retire. And my sons tell me, \"When you gonna retire?\"."], [0, "Why?"], [2, "I'm too young to retire."], [1, "Dr. Medina first learned about Los Algodones from his brother-in-law, Dr. Bernardo Maga\u00f1a, who's often described as the godfather of Molar City."], [2, "He was the first dentist from this town, you know?"], [1, "Maga\u00f1a's now in his 80s. He is retired and didn't want to be interviewed. But Medina told me about how his brother-in-law Maga\u00f1a first had this vision to transform the town into a dental mecca. Back in the '60s, Maga\u00f1a and Medina were living together in a city about 25 miles from Algodones. Maga\u00f1a was a young dentist looking for a place to set up his private practice. At the time, he was working for someone who already had a few American patients. Patients who likely lived in the South and would cross into Mexico to take advantage of lower prices."], [2, "Gladstone started to tell about Algodones. He says, \"Why don't you move to Algodones? Because Algodones is 4 minutes from Yuma.\" He says, \"If you go there, you're going to have a lot more patients.\"."], [1, "At first, Maga\u00f1a was skeptical. He paid Los Algodones a visit, and it was a lot like he expected. A sleepy little place with a lot of mostly drunk people. But many of those drunk people were Americans. Its proximity to the border meant there was already a culture of coming and going. He saw the Americans spending their money at the bars and thought maybe they'd spend money on dentistry too. Maga\u00f1a knew he could offer them a price lower than what they could get in America."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["small town", "cantinas", "yuma", "border crossing", "mexico", "los algodones", "dentist", "doctor", "retirement", "dental clinic", "american patients", "lower prices", "medical tourism", "private practice", "healthcare industry", "entrepreneurship", "regional economy", "cultural exchange"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709043.4908006, "duration_sec": 3.219386339187622, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And he said, I've heard about that and I've warned about it, but I haven't really seen any evidence of it. And so therefore, we can continue to engage with China. So we still have a major league problem inside the intelligence community, inside the Pentagon. I think it's less so in the Pentagon today. I mean, I think the admirals and the people that are inside the service understand that China is a threat, but it's everything that's led from the top down and that the president..."], [0, "and the National Security Council, if they're not directing the nation to address this threat and get everybody aligned on the same sheet of music, then we have a problem."], [0, "Brad, a specific example in your book, CFIUS, which is a committee in Washington that reviews the possible national security consequences of the sale of a company, an enterprise, an intellectual property to a foreign power. CFIUS, in specific, vouchsafed the purchase of sustainable material technology from DuPont bought by a Chinese company."], [0, "You're recommending that CFIUS now under the Department of Commerce be moved to the Department of Defense because selling China intellectual property, given the repeated demonstrations of how they've stolen everything that doesn't exactly fall under your hand, you know, you have to keep your hand on it. It'll be stolen from you."], [0, "You recommend moving to the DOD. Is Washington ready to do that, to approach this problem as, as Jim just said, whole of government?"], [0, "John, good evening to you, of course, and to Jim. Again, it's wonderful to have the opportunity to talk about these very important topics. I don't believe so, John, not under the Biden administration. Moving CFIUS to the Department of Defense would be an important step in bringing about greater control over exports. But as you observe, there are so many legal and illegal measures that"], [0, "that the Chinese Communist Party employs through its entities, as well as people within the United States, partners, of course, and others within the United States. The whole of government solution, whole of society solution that we've touched on is not yet present, John, sad to say. And what we need to start talking about seriously are not key measures like CFIUS,"], [0, "and attempting to target something very specific, but to begin to open the door to cutting off trade and investment in total with the People's Republic of China. And as a measure of the whole of government, whole of society response, beginning for business communities, the Chamber of Commerce, Wall Street, and of course the Hill, and the departments and agencies to recognize, as Jim said, that this is the enemy."], [0, "And we need to harden ourselves and protect our property, intellectual property as well as physical property. And we need to have our allies do the same. And the sand in the hourglass is definitely low now. We're really in a situation where these steps must be taken urgently if we're to have any chance of dealing with the threat, with the urgency and taking the necessary measures."], [0, "Moving past four and five, Jim, you want me to be optimistic? I'm not after reading your book. So I'm move past five action now. Yes, that's evident. We need to take action now. But what action? So what is team B? How did that function in the first Cold War, Jim?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["china threat", "national security", "intelligence community", "pentagon", "national security council", "cfius", "foreign investment review", "intellectual property", "technology transfer", "export controls", "trade restrictions", "whole of government", "commerce department", "defense department", "cold war", "allies coordination", "corporate lobbying", "policy reform"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709323.6634336, "duration_sec": 1.8867971897125244, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "This is pretty scary. First of all, you're putting the data at risk by having it in some random personal system. And second, it seems pretty obvious that you're trying to hide your conversation. So I don't know how this is even legal or how you just file for an exception for that. Seems highly suspect to me, and it's quite interesting that it happened. Seems like this is the type of thing you would specifically try to avoid"], [0, "if you're trying to put on some sort of air of transparency. That was something that Obama talked about is transparency. How do you hire a secretary of state and not require that her email is visible to citizens? Pretty interesting. So Facebook can access your account without your password. This is good to know. Evidently, any Facebook employee"], [0, "can pivot over and be inside of your account. And I don't know if that's an exaggeration or if there's some sort of filter that needs to be in place. I imagine after all the press they received, there's probably some sort of filter in place now. But a little bit interesting to see that that was possible. So Google no longer encrypting Lollipop by default."], [0, "It was basically one of the main selling points for the new version of Android, and now it seems to have basically gone away. They said it was a driver issue. Basically, they can't quite pull it off yet, which kind of makes sense, doesn't sound malicious or anything, but unfortunate that this was going to be a major feature that is no longer there. So D-Link routers have a remote command injection bug."], [0, "that could allow for DNS hijacking other attacks. I'm not sure if this is cross-site scripting or CSERF. I didn't look into it, but I imagine it's one of those two. And the key thing is it's being done over the internet and basically compromising a lot of different systems. It seems like there are quite a few of these actually out there right now, routers getting owned from a distance."], [0, "It kind of plays into the whole IoT thing, what happens when you're exposing lots of the same type of system to the internet. Basically, you just need something like Shodan or Scan.io or Sonar from Rapid7, basically to find one instance, one vulnerability, and then look for the whole internet to see who's running that same vulnerable version. And now you have a mass compromise."], [0, "And it's only going to get worse when, you know, trees and baby clothes and soccer balls are all online, right? That's going to be a bigger problem. So ISIS has threatened some members of the Twitter team for disabling their accounts. I believe they went after an executive and a couple of staff members. Really highlights the whole concept of like OPSEC and personal privacy and"], [0, "what you expose online. You know, these people might have had public sort of personas and maybe they talked about where they lived and stuff like that and didn't care about it. But now suddenly they're disabling ISIS accounts and ISIS are able to just kind of know their whereabouts, know who they're married to and frightening stuff. I think on a personal level, I'm a strong proponent of the belief that"], [0, "the way to avoid attack is to avoid being a target, right? If you're not angering, if you're not, you know, kicking the, the, the hornet's nest or whatever, um, you're not a target. That is the way to avoid being attacked. And you could be basically as public as you want. And the worst you're going to get, if you're running an average blog is, you know, uh, people on 4chan, uh,"], [0, "talking shit and being rude and stuff like that. But it's not going to devolve into actual safety issues. But the moment you start, you're in a field where you expose yourself to some kind of major scrutiny like that. Like I've got friends who do threat intelligence and InfoSec and basically track down and sick the authorities on really rich and powerful and"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["data security", "email privacy", "government transparency", "facebook access", "account security", "android encryption", "google lollipop", "router vulnerabilities", "command injection", "dns hijacking", "internet of things", "mass scanning", "cybersecurity", "social media threats", "operational security", "rapid7", "online anonymity", "risk management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
