{"timestamp": 1778709537.9238336, "duration_sec": 5.121988296508789, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "South Platte River\nCommittee\nMinutes\nWednesday, April 22, 2026, 1:30 PM\nChair: Jamie Torres, Vice Chair: Sarah Parady\nMembers: Flor Alvidrez, Kevin Flynn, Chris Hinds, Amanda Sandoval, Darrell Watson\nStaff: Melissa Mata\nAttendance\nPresent 6 - Alvidrez, Flynn, Sandoval, Watson, Chair Jamie Torres, Romero Campbell\nAbsent 2 - Hinds, Vice Chair Sarah Parady\nAction Items\n26-0521 Approves a map amendment to rezone property from I-A, UO-2 to C-MX-8,\nlocated at 361-363 West Evans Avenue, in Council District 7.\nA motion offered by Council member Alvidrez, duly seconded by Council\nPresident Sandoval, that Council Bill 26-0521 be approved for filing, carried by\nthe following vote:\nAye: 5 - Alvidrez, Flynn, Sandoval, Watson, Chair Torres\nNay: 0\nAbsent: 2 - Hinds, Vice Chair Parady\nCity and County of Denver Page 1 Printed on 4/22/2026"], [0, "This presentation will take a deep dive into Denver\u2019s ...\nWe will walk through the entire typical rezoning process, and we will do this by using an example of a rezoning case to understand the review criteria. And we will review the steps of all those involved, including the steps for applicants, neighbors, City staff, Planning Board, and City Council."], [1, "Rezonings - Map Amendments - City and County of Denver\nClick on the map below to look at current proposed rezoning cases or search by address."], [2, "Zone District Descriptions and Definitions - City ... - Denver\nTo understand zone districts with waivers and conditions, look up the zone district in the Denver Zoning Code or Former Chapter 59 according to the listed instructions for the baseline information on the district."], [3, "Commercial Construction Cost Per Square Foot - Digital Builder\nMay 19, 2025 \u00b7 Due to climate differences, the building costs in the US Midwest vary. However, the expenses are averaged from three cities in the Midwest: Denver , Nashville, and Chicago."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["city and county of denver", "denver", "denver city council", "denver planning board", "south platte river committee", "council bill 26-0521", "denver zoning code", "former chapter 59", "jamie torres", "sarah parady", "flor alvidrez", "amanda sandoval", "kevin flynn", "chris hinds", "digital builder", "nashville", "chicago", "us midwest", "361-363 west evans avenue", "council district 7"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709930.7103934, "duration_sec": 1.8117308616638184, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Almost all of us who are listening to this episode right now, we all use generic languages for this very reason. We use languages that can generalize to many different problem sets. And this is especially true for people who are in typical programming, software engineering jobs."], [0, "This becomes less true depending on how specialized your area of study is, how specialized your area of engineering is, but it is certainly true for the vast majority of the industry. Now, the last thing I want to talk about is the job."], [0, "What exactly is the job? And more specifically, I want to talk about an error that we often make when we're trying to pick the right tool for the job. Very often, we focus on the right tool part of this advice. But actually, the thing that we have wrong, the thing that we didn't define very well, is the job part. So we might be picking the right tool for the wrong job."], [0, "If we don't understand what needs to be done, then it's easy to quickly jump to conclusions about the problem."], [0, "Now, this doesn't mean that you have to specify everything about the problem in order to make some kind of reasonable decisions. Really, all we're trying to do is predict some kind of fit, predict some kind of effectiveness of a tool in a given problem set. But it does make sense to develop a consistent language when describing your problems."], [0, "And this isn't a new concept. In fact, one of the early pioneers in computer science, Michael Jackson, he wrote a book about something called problem frames. Problem frames is a way of identifying a class of problems. Of course, he goes on to identify specific types of problem frames, but"], [0, "But the specifics of that, of course, have become more complex, and it's necessary for us to gain this kind of language in our modern software engineering context. And the reason for this is that if you don't understand the general idea, the kind of pattern of problem that you're dealing with, it's very possible, if not likely, that you're going to choose a tool based on what you wish the problem was."], [0, "And when I say wish here, I don't mean that you are thinking about it and hoping, you know, very excited that this problem is this particular thing, but rather that you're using some kind of intuitive fitting of what you think the problem is to the tool that you think will solve the problem."], [0, "Many of us have experienced this phenomenon as engineers. The moment we hear the beginning of a problem, not even the full initial pitch for a problem, we start imagining solutions to the problem. And there's nothing necessarily wrong with this, except if we're too quick to jump to tool picking problems,"], [0, "We may be picking the tools for the wrong job. So develop this sense and the language that you need to describe your problems. One way to do this is to look into these kind of pattern languages like Michael Jackson actually wrote about. Another way to do this is to talk about these problems in terms of what are they similar to? What kinds of problems is this problem similar to?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["programming languages", "generic languages", "software engineering", "tool selection", "problem definition", "problem solving", "solution design", "problem patterns", "pattern language", "problem frames", "requirements analysis", "engineering specialization", "computer science", "decision making", "cognitive bias", "systems thinking", "technology industry", "communication skills"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778710079.6325614, "duration_sec": 1.7203028202056885, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And instead, I realized that intentional design, whether it's a space or a culture, is so powerful in creating and evoking an emotion. Starbucks, the music changes based on the time of day. The lighting changes based on the time of day. And that's all, again, for an emotional response."], [0, "That intersection for me, this research into psychology and then again being sort of face-to-face with, I think, amazingly executed design, I started drawing little charts around my feelings and trying to visualize those. And that was sometimes cartoons of here's what it feels like to be anxious. As I then went on to start applying for other jobs, I drew charts around here's a job interview in charts and here's what I'm thinking, here's probably what the person who's interviewing me thinking. And"], [0, "I had a couple of those projects go viral, and I think that was for me the first signal of, oh, the visual aspect of this can be a really effective way of communicating these universal emotions and making them more engaging and accessible."], [0, "Around this time, Liz moved to New York and was looking for new friends. I sent an email to everyone I knew asking to be set up on platonic friend dates, and Molly was the first platonic friend, and we just had this instant connection. We wrote some articles together about what it feels like to be an introvert, both within life and at work, and just loved collaborating with Liz on that. And I think"], [0, "Liz's illustrations do such a great job of visualizing things that are hard to verbalize, like emotions and like what it feels like to be an introvert. And so I just thought, oh, I want to do more of this collaboration with her, with her illustrations. It just seemed natural. We both had these really common interests, had had common experiences around emotion in the workplace."], [0, "And so started collaborating on things and that was co-authoring and then also adding illustrations. And yeah, I think also just kind of realizing that working with her, it was never stressful. I just felt a lot of positive emotions around it. So that to me, too, was a really strong signal that we might be able to work on a bigger project very effectively together."], [0, "You had one line in your book that's pretty prominent, and I feel like this is what resonated with me the most. And it was the future of work is emotional. Can you talk about what that means? We have thought a lot about how work is going to change in the future. And there's"], [0, "two things that we think are going to change um one is that we are going to be more and more collaborative and if you are more and more collaborative you are working in groups with other people and so it just you need to have a better understanding of your own emotions so you can communicate them and they aren't coming out in"], [0, "bad ways, unexpected ways, and understanding others' emotions as well. The other big change is that more and more the workplace is becoming digital, and as the world becomes more digital,"], [0, "It's harder to communicate emotions because you're stripped away of facial expressions, body language, ways that we normally pick up on others' emotions. And so we have to try even harder to communicate the emotions in text, email, Slack, video conference. But..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["intentional design", "workplace culture", "emotional response", "psychology research", "data visualization", "illustrations", "universal emotions", "anxiety", "job interviews", "introversion", "collaboration", "future of work", "user experience", "brand strategy", "digital communication", "remote work", "emotional intelligence", "nonverbal cues"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
